#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d7ae17-2c3d-59ec-ba2c-3e1b1ece9b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} {:answer/id #uuid "9844962c-c7e2-5ca4-b168-d25ddd968bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382626} {:answer/id #uuid "7bad8b18-c3b7-57e1-94f1-0e4413f803e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382627} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5d21c8-5d93-532f-bba2-c9750176bd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382628} {:answer/id #uuid "ff46a24f-327b-502f-9d90-4a8afbd8cf9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bad8b18-c3b7-57e1-94f1-0e4413f803e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382627} :answer-field/id #uuid "a554af39-db97-5574-b248-19769eb04830" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0dfbc45b-5714-518b-b41d-f57772769822" :question/math-academy-id "q-140133" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]C=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $C:$ \n\n$$\nC={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $C$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3458a6f-9c67-5e9e-bdae-7d70f0a69fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306826} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f2d51f-b847-5d55-b18a-a42942fcc046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306827} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea77758-35fa-5d1a-93fd-3e88e2d58ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306828} {:answer/id #uuid "29d4effd-0ff3-59ae-9e45-b14b22ffc070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306829} {:answer/id #uuid "75d71b7a-85b3-515f-8ff9-f8415953f187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3458a6f-9c67-5e9e-bdae-7d70f0a69fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306826} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2003b6f-0454-5988-8299-6a6315503fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1eed05-d07c-590e-b279-39088f439f63" :question/math-academy-id "q-37362" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $N$ given that \n\n$$\nN[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $N:$\n\n$$\nN={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $N$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec6580b8-209a-50ef-b975-08cb7bc3c8cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}70 & 32 \\\\ 32 & -52\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382632} {:answer/id #uuid "1f6cec0c-40b9-5843-8c3d-862a61ad1efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382633} {:answer/id #uuid "ee13eda4-1186-580f-9f68-adb8991a8cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382634} {:answer/id #uuid "881127bc-79dc-56a3-9586-0d20b6219db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382635} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fd5fec-f8a0-5658-9e1d-17dbe9ce852e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}70 & -13 \\\\ 17 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f6cec0c-40b9-5843-8c3d-862a61ad1efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382633} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a7736fc-c5be-532c-940f-debed8cb49ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "260f4a93-7ac0-5b56-be41-90f5a4009ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37630" :question/problem "Find ${N}^{18}$ where\n\n$$\nN=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $N.$\n\n$$\nN={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $N=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${N}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{N}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{18} & =P{D}^{18}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{18}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[9]{2})^{18}\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 20 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "947d3107-ce6e-5a5c-b7a3-97a70b321f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382639} {:answer/id #uuid "ceff64b1-b502-5883-bc69-37c5207ef45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382640} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1c2078-88b7-5522-8b5c-deb36348c186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382641} {:answer/id #uuid "9e118059-cd9b-57bd-9faf-870e70c305e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382642} {:answer/id #uuid "c9128c97-9490-5637-a2ad-b8ad853adede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "947d3107-ce6e-5a5c-b7a3-97a70b321f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382639} :answer-field/id #uuid "6661e1fd-afa1-5e76-a9b3-f8a3eef6cc7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bac7350-7ccc-5944-ad6d-0daace45e031" :question/math-academy-id "q-86686" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{85},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{85} & =P{D}^{85}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{85}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{85} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{85}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cda199f-919c-56ac-bd4a-2147b49bacee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -28 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382646} {:answer/id #uuid "74c78b71-7b4a-532f-bdc8-e4559b5da860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 60 \\\\ -18 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382647} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c4a3dc-5e56-5477-9b3e-da4ecf71b29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-60 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382648} {:answer/id #uuid "e366dca6-8298-5fc1-be57-ecf1a3ecf75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382649} {:answer/id #uuid "be8cf13f-256c-5791-9556-d378ad24b045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be8cf13f-256c-5791-9556-d378ad24b045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382650} :answer-field/id #uuid "a749b3a2-302b-5c2e-9bad-08654f213458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee77777a-134d-53f5-9b84-4266354b8ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86689" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{24},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{24}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
